      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their cited evidence at the correct tier. Stated claims quote directly from policy text. Measurable claims (E5, E7) cite parliamentary/government sources for baseline facts. Projected claims (E22, E23, E21) are appropriately flagged as projections with advocacy/media sources labelled. No tier inflation detected. Direction 'worsens' is well-supported by the three stated coercive instruments (mandatory bodily screening, variable financial mandate, surcharge increase). Magnitude 'moderate' is reasonable given the liberty effect is confined to migrants not settled population, and the biggest_unknown about threshold operationalisation is properly flagged. No party-blind issues \u2014 rationale judges the mechanism, not the proposer's motives. No false balance \u2014 the verdict does not claim mixed/uncertain when evidence points one way. Lever_leakage is absent \u2014 the verdict judges the coercive mechanisms' effect on O10 directly, not immigration policy as a lever for economic or health outcomes. Minor observation: E21 concerns 'overseas visitors' distinct from IHS payers per the evidence text itself ('who are distinct from IHS payers'), so the claim that 'past upfront-charge policies have led some migrants to decline treatment' conflates two different groups. However, the claim is framed as 'comparable upfront-charge regimes' in the rationale and the evidence quote does support the factual claim as stated (migrants declining treatment due to cost fear). This is a minor contextual nuance, not a blocking source_support failure, as the broader point about chilling effects on healthcare-seeking remains supported."
